#!/bin/sh
# Turn on the ufw firewall. ufw's defaults block incoming connections, allow
# all outgoing traffic, and (in /etc/ufw/before.rules) allow DHCP replies plus
# mDNS and UPnP discovery, so printers and network services are still found.
#
# ufw can't load its rules inside the build chroot, so only the setting is
# changed here; ufw.service applies the rules at boot.
# Users manage it with: sudo ufw status | allow PORT | disable
set -e

if [ -f /etc/ufw/ufw.conf ]; then
    sed -i 's/^ENABLED=.*/ENABLED=yes/' /etc/ufw/ufw.conf
    # Record the same answer in debconf, which ufw's maintainer scripts use.
    echo "ufw ufw/enable boolean true" | debconf-set-selections
    systemctl enable ufw.service
fi
